\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-B}}Reads consumed versus capacity reserved}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-actual}{{\mbox  {VI-B}}{8}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {V}{\ignorespaces Reads \emph  {consumed} with verified stopping versus capacity that must be \emph  {reserved} without it (digital twin, 300 retrievals per row). All rows stop on verification, so the comparison between rows is allocation only; the last column is the budget the same read proportions would need without stopping, which coincides with q99 wherever every retrieval succeeds. Thousands of reads; mean over all attempts with a cluster-bootstrap 95\% interval.}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:actual}{{V}{9}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-C}}Policy comparison on the digital twin}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {VI}{\ignorespaces Digital-twin end-to-end retrieval (DT4DDS; primary evidence). Per scenario and policy: share of retrievals whose output file is byte-identical to the stored file (no retrieval returned a wrong file), mean reads per retrieval (thousands; cluster-bootstrap 95\% CI over syntheses), the 0.99-quantile q99 (the budget to reserve), and the paired relative difference in mean reads against round-robin with verified stopping on retrievals where both succeeded (95\% CI). \textbf  {Static plans are costed here as \emph  {reservations}}: they read fixed proportions until decodable and must commit their q99 on every retrieval, so their mean cost equals their q99, and their proportions use per-container efficiencies measured in hindsight on the same scenario. Table~\ref {tab:actual} reports the same plans run \emph  {with} verified stopping, where their consumed mean is far lower; the two tables answer different questions and their static rows are not comparable.}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:dt}{{VI}{10}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-D}}What the gain after a container failure buys}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-uncond}{{\mbox  {VI-D}}{10}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Digital-twin end-to-end retrieval, baseline unless stated. \textbf  {(a)} For each policy, the reads a retrieval actually \emph  {consumes}: filled circles are the mean over 300 retrievals with a 95\% cluster-bootstrap interval over syntheses, open squares the 0.99-quantile of the same retrievals. The two vertical lines are the capacity a plan that does \emph  {not} stop must \emph  {reserve} to succeed on 99\% of retrievals: dotted for uniform fixed depth, dashed for the best static plan. The horizontal distance from a filled circle to a vertical line is the saving verified stopping provides; the distance between circles is the (much smaller) allocation effect. \textbf  {(b)} Allocation effect alone: each bar is the paired difference in mean reads against round-robin with the same verified stopping, computed on the retrievals in which both policies succeeded, with 95\% cluster-bootstrap intervals; negative is cheaper than round-robin. Information-greedy is annotated rather than plotted because it runs off the scale. Scenarios in which no retrieval succeeded (photolithographic, two failed containers) have no paired statistic and are omitted.}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dt}{{2}{11}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {VII}{\ignorespaces One failed container: cost conditional on success and over all attempts (300 retrievals per row, 72k-read stream). Every policy has the same success probability, so the conditional saving does not buy a better chance of recovery, and failures dominate the unconditional mean. Thousands of reads.}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:uncond}{{VII}{11}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-E}}Why adaptive allocation loses, and what fixes it}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-misspec}{{\mbox  {VI-E}}{11}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-F}}An efficiency-and-dropout container model}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-dropout}{{\mbox  {VI-F}}{11}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {VIII}{\ignorespaces Ablation of the three modelling choices (digital twin; paired mean reads against round-robin on jointly successful retrievals, per cent, negative is cheaper than round-robin). $\theta $: one efficiency multiplier per container (the frozen model); $\theta {+}d$: efficiency and a dropout fraction. $\rho _U$ is the beta-binomial correlation and $s$ the prior width; the frozen configuration is the first column. The penalty is large only in that column: relaxing any one of the three choices moves every rule into a band a few per cent below round-robin. Blank: not run. Cluster-bootstrap intervals for every cell are in \texttt  {results/r4\_ablation.csv}; those the text relies on are quoted there.}}{12}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:r4abl}{{VIII}{12}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-G}}Failed containers and severity sweep}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-H}}Tail metrics and the static family}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-tail}{{\mbox  {VI-H}}{13}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Severity sweep: mean reads consumed as the degraded container's copy number falls from 200 to 1 (300 retrievals per point and policy, 95\% cluster-bootstrap intervals; the axis runs from mild to severe degradation). Every retrieval at every level returned the correct file, so the success probability is 1.00 throughout and is not plotted. Adaptive rules are cheaper than round-robin only at the mild end.}}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sweep}{{3}{13}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {IX}{\ignorespaces Tail metrics with cluster-bootstrap 95\% intervals over synthesis replicates (baseline). Extreme quantiles estimated from a few hundred retrievals are imprecise, and the intervals of most policies overlap; the ordering of the means does not carry over to q99. Thousands of reads.}}{14}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:r4tails}{{IX}{14}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-I}}Calibration of the posterior, and a negative forecast result}{14}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-calib}{{\mbox  {VI-I}}{14}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {X}{\ignorespaces Re-run of the $\rho _U$ selection with the prior actually used in the policy runs ($s{=}0.5$) and an extended grid: coverage of the 90\% credible interval of $\theta ^\ast _j$, overall and per container. No value in the grid reaches 0.90 overall, and no value covers the near-dead container.}}{15}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:r4calib}{{X}{15}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-J}}Secondary evidence: real read streams}{15}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-replay}{{\mbox  {VI-J}}{15}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {XI}{\ignorespaces Secondary evidence: semi-empirical replay of real Illumina read streams (ENA PRJEB65931), generic prior, no evaluation-split statistics. Mean reads (thousands) and q99; $\Delta $: mean minus the hindsight-tuned static budget (paired bootstrap 95\% CI). Decodability is an oracle event here (consensus equals the designed strand), so this is \emph  {not} exact retrieval.}}{15}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:replay}{{XI}{15}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Is the posterior over container efficiency calibrated? Each point or bar is the fraction of cases in which the 90\% credible interval for $\theta _j$ contained the target $\theta ^\ast _j$; a calibrated posterior would sit on the dotted line at 0.90. \textbf  {(a)} Selection replicates 1000--1009, one curve per container, against the beta-binomial correlation $\rho _U$; the thick black curve is the mean over containers, which is what the selection rule maximised. \textbf  {(b)} Held-out replicates 0--29 at the selected $\rho _U{=}0.1$, per container. Five containers are covered perfectly and the near-dead one is covered in 0.20 of cases at this $\rho _U$ (0.00--0.03 across the extended grid of Table~\ref {tab:r4calib}): no value of $\rho _U$ calibrates the container that matters.}}{16}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:calib}{{4}{16}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Reliability of the completion forecast on the digital twin: forecast probability of finishing within the remaining budget against the observed frequency of finishing, pooled over checkpoints at 30/50/70/85\% of the budget (10 equal-width bins, bins with fewer than five checkpoints dropped). A calibrated forecast would lie on the diagonal; both curves lie below it, so both are over-confident, and both have a worse Brier score than a constant forecast at the base rate.}}{16}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:forecast}{{5}{16}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {XII}{\ignorespaces What the calibrated prior costs (Genscript replay, coverage-greedy). Calibration reads are charged explicitly: they are the first reads of the same six sequencing runs, disjoint from the evaluation reads. Break-even is the number of retrievals over which the calibration reads are repaid by the per-retrieval saving against round-robin. Thousands of reads.}}{16}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:r4calcost}{{XII}{16}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-K}}Robustness: what does and does not change the conclusions}{16}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-robust}{{\mbox  {VI-K}}{16}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {XIII}{\ignorespaces Sensitivity to the consensus cap, the number of reads per cluster used to form a consensus (frozen value 24). On the baseline the cap is not binding: clusters rarely reach a dozen reads before the fragment decodes. Thousands of reads.}}{17}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:r4cap}{{XIII}{17}{}{}{}}
